    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d224">

                <pb facs="0182" n="170" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg170"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.20 Defense/19<hi rendition="#superscript"
                        >13/14</hi>: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Uruguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Montevideo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-10-29T18:00:00-03:00">October 29, 1940—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 29—5:37 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>248. My 241, October 22, 2 p.m. Lieutenant Colonel Christian and Captain Brereton
                    leave today for Buenos Aires, the former proceeding tomorrow by air for the
                    United States.</p>
                <p>The conversations here have been highly satisfactory. Future developments,
                    however, and the maintenance of the existing cordial attitude on the part of
                    Uruguay as regards cooperation with the <pb facs="0183" n="171" xml:id="pg171"/>
                    United States in questions of continental defense will depend in large measure
                    upon our willingness and ability to make available to Uruguay certain essential
                    military and naval material.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
